    "kernel_code": "1: \"\"\"Sequency-ordered integer Walsh-Hadamard transform.\n2: \n3: Original synthetic Triton implementation of a standard signal transform.\n4: This is not an upstream implementation or a claimed historical defect.\n5: \"\"\"\n6: import torch\n7: import triton\n8: import triton.language as tl\n9: \n10: \n11: @triton.jit\n12: def _transform_kernel(X, OUT, N: tl.constexpr, LOG_N: tl.constexpr):\n13:     row = tl.program_id(0)\n14:     lane = tl.arange(0, N)\n15:     value = tl.load(X + row * N + lane).to(tl.int32)\n16:     for stage in tl.static_range(0, LOG_N):\n17:         step = 1 << stage\n18:         peer = tl.gather(value, lane ^ step, axis=0)\n19:         value = tl.where((lane & step) == 0, value + peer, peer - value)\n20: \n21:     # Convert the coefficient index to the butterfly's natural row index.\n22:     gray = lane ^ (lane >> 1)\n23:     source_lane = tl.full((N,), 0, tl.int32)\n24:     for bit in tl.static_range(0, LOG_N):\n25:         source_lane = source_lane | (((gray >> bit) & 1) << (LOG_N - 1 - bit))\n26: \n27:     result = tl.gather(value, source_lane, axis=0)\n28:     tl.store(OUT + row * N + lane, result)\n29: \n30: \n31: def validate_inputs(x, require_cuda=True):\n32:     \"\"\"Validate the complete public domain; also usable on CPU.\"\"\"\n33:     def require(condition, message):\n34:         if not condition:\n35:             raise ValueError(message)\n36: \n37:     require(not torch.is_inference_mode_enabled(), \"ordinary execution mode required\")\n38:     require(type(x) is torch.Tensor, \"ordinary torch.Tensor required\")\n39:     require(x.layout == torch.strided and x.is_contiguous(),\n40:             \"contiguous strided input required\")\n41:     require(not x.requires_grad and not x.is_inference(),\n42:             \"ordinary non-gradient input required\")\n43:     require(not x.is_neg() and not x.is_conj(),\n44:             \"logical view flags are outside this domain\")\n45:     require(not require_cuda or x.is_cuda, \"CUDA input required by run\")\n46:     require(x.ndim == 2 and 1 <= x.shape[0] <= 128 and x.shape[1] in (32, 64, 128),\n47:             \"x must have shape [B,N], B in [1,128] and N in {32,64,128}\")\n48:     require(x.dtype == torch.int16, \"int16 input required\")\n49:     return {\"valid\": True, \"batch\": x.shape[0], \"width\": x.shape[1]}\n50: \n51: \n52: def run(x):\n53:     \"\"\"Return the unnormalized transform with coefficients in sequency order.\"\"\"\n54:     validate_inputs(x)\n55:     batch, width = x.shape\n56:     with torch.cuda.device(x.device):\n57:         output = torch.empty((batch, width), dtype=torch.int32, device=x.device)\n58:         _transform_kernel[(batch,)](\n59:             x, output, N=width, LOG_N=width.bit_length() - 1, num_warps=4)\n60:     return output\n61: \n62: \n63: def reference(x):\n64:     \"\"\"Build the mathematical basis independently, then multiply in int64.\"\"\"\n65:     validate_inputs(x, require_cuda=False)\n66:     width = x.shape[1]\n67:     hadamard = torch.ones((1, 1), dtype=torch.int64, device=x.device)\n68:     while hadamard.shape[0] < width:\n69:         top = torch.cat((hadamard, hadamard), dim=1)\n70:         bottom = torch.cat((hadamard, -hadamard), dim=1)\n71:         hadamard = torch.cat((top, bottom), dim=0)\n72:     changes = (hadamard[:, 1:] != hadamard[:, :-1]).sum(dim=1)\n73:     sequency_rows = hadamard[torch.argsort(changes)]\n74:     # int64 products and sums are exact throughout the complete int16 domain.\n75:     result = (x.to(torch.int64)[:, None, :] * sequency_rows[None, :, :]).sum(dim=2)\n76:     return result.to(torch.int32)\n77: \n78: \n79: def make_inputs(device=\"cuda\", batch=7, width=64, seed=0):\n80:     \"\"\"Examples only; all stored int16 values are legal.\"\"\"\n81:     generator = torch.Generator(device=\"cpu\").manual_seed(seed)\n82:     x = torch.randint(-32768, 32768, (batch, width), generator=generator, dtype=torch.int32)\n83:     return (x.to(device=device, dtype=torch.int16),)",
    "passed": "unknown",
    "problem_text": "Compute an unnormalized Walsh-Hadamard transform for each row of an int16\ninput tensor, returning int32 coefficients in increasing sequency order.\n\nThe mathematical target is defined independently of any fast algorithm:\n1. Start with H_1 = [[1]] and recursively construct\n       H_(2n) = [[H_n, H_n],\n                 [H_n, -H_n]].\n2. For each row of H_N, count how many adjacent entries have different\n   signs. These counts are all distinct and range from 0 to N-1.\n3. Sort the rows by that count, in ascending order, to obtain W_N.\n4. Return out[b,k] = sum_j W_N[k,j] * x[b,j].\n\nThus output coefficient k uses a basis row with exactly k sign changes.\nNo normalization factor is applied, and output order is part of correctness.\n\nInputs and complete public domain:\n- x is an ordinary torch.Tensor (not a subclass), with dtype int16 and\n  shape [B,N], where 1<=B<=128 and N is one of {32,64,128}.\n- EVERY stored int16 value is legal: the full range [-32768,32767].\n- x is dense, strided, contiguous and on a CUDA device. Contiguous views\n  with nonzero storage offsets are legal.\n- x has requires_grad=False, is_inference=False, is_neg=False and\n  is_conj=False. Calls run in ordinary non-inference mode, without\n  concurrent external writes to the input. No autograd is provided.\n\nReturn a fresh int32 tensor of shape [B,N] on x.device, preserving x.\nEvery element must equal the mathematical integer target exactly:\nabsolute tolerance = 0 and relative tolerance = 0.\nThe largest possible absolute result is at most N*32768<=4194304, which\nfits in int32. Approximate transforms, other coefficient orderings and\nnormalized transforms do not satisfy this task. Implementations may use\nany algorithm that satisfies the same target over the whole legal domain.\nCorrectness is one verdict for the implementation, not a verdict per input,\ninput distribution, seed or size.\n\nvalidate_inputs() enforces this domain and is called by run(). reference()\nbuilds the Sylvester matrix, sorts its rows by sign-change count and uses\nint64 products and sums before exact int32 conversion; it also accepts\nCPU inputs. make_inputs() supplies examples only.\n\nWalsh-Hadamard transforms are used for discrete signals and their sequency\nordering arranges coefficients by the sign-change rate of the basis.\nThis is an original synthetic Triton implementation with integer\nbutterflies and coefficient reordering, not a claimed upstream historical\ndefect. The input/output convention is explicitly unnormalized.\n\nFrozen environment: NVIDIA T4, PyTorch2.8.0, Triton3.4.0.\n",
